Z||000009||fieronv||12-04-2001||06:54 PM||||Okay now for the real answer. First tap the monitor lightly and see if the color comes back momentaraly. If it does take the back off and you'll see a board attached to the end of the picture tube. Remove the board and unsolder any sheilding from the board (you want to be able to see the solder side of the board. Look at the circle of solder pads around the center of the board (the "white" connector) resolder all the pads adding new solder. If this does not fix the problem it can be one of three things. <BR>1. Your video cable is bad.<BR>2. one of the transistors that control the color is bad.<BR>3. You lost a color "gun" in the tube. <BR>Good Luck and let us know how you did.<P>Don<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2_WEB_1.jpg">   <BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||1
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-04-2001||07:09 PM||||BE CAREFUL!   <P>there are no serviceable parts for the homeuser inside a monitor.<P>It would be cheaper to toss it and get a new one.. <P>(I wouldnt trust the monitor, it could short out)<P>A friend was watching tv once and his monitor started smoking..he unplugged and tossed it outside just in time. <P>1. NEVER leave your monitor on when your not there..<BR>2. try to get another asap (is it worth a fire?)<P>I find from working around computers, monitors have a 2-3 year lifespan.<P>Good luck!<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieronv:<BR><B>Okay now for the real answer. First tap the monitor lightly and see if the color comes back momentaraly. If it does take the back off and you'll see a board attached to the end of the picture tube. Remove the board and unsolder any sheilding from the board (you want to be able to see the solder side of the board. Look at the circle of solder pads around the center of the board (the "white" connector) resolder all the pads adding new solder. If this does not fix the problem it can be one of three things. <BR>1. Your video cable is bad.<BR>2. one of the transistors that control the color is bad.<BR>3. You lost a color "gun" in the tube. <BR>Good Luck and let us know how you did.<P>Don<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieronv||12-04-2001||10:47 PM||||On modern monitors they have a circuit that  turns the monitor to stand by when there is no signal(yellow power light) this turns off all the high voltage in the monitor. I ave been repairing monitors for 15 years and have only had two monitors that i've seen that had cuaght on fire. Even if it does go up in "flames" you don't have to worry about explosion the tubes have implosion protection that will stop it from hurting anything. Also you have to keep you hand off of the high voltage line (red wire that attaches to the tube) when the monitor is on. <BR>The reason I mentioned the repair that I did is becuase in the years that I have repaired monitors this is the most common fix for the description of the problem. After doing the repair I have had these monitors last up to 3-4 years. A decent monitor that is taken care of (blowing the dust out and not turn the brightness up to much) will last along the lines of 5-8 years.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2_WEB_1.jpg">   <BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula||12-04-2001||11:24 PM||||try wiggling the cable... thats how I fix the ones that turn yellow at work!<P>sometimes a pin gets bent somehow and it turns it yellow or green.  play with the cable first.||||reg||1
